# Project Context: Evaluation of Adaptive Optics in FSO Communication

## Overview
**Title:** Evaluation of Adaptive Optics for Correcting Atmospheric Distortions in Free-Space Optical Communication
**Author:** Dongbeom Son
**Date:** January 22, 2025 (Experiment: Oct 2024 - Dec 2024)

## Abstract
This project evaluates the effectiveness of a closed-loop Adaptive Optics (AO) system in mitigating atmospheric wavefront distortions for Free-Space Optical (FSO) communication. Using a heating wire to simulate turbulence, the study compares signal intensity and Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) with and without AO correction.

## Key Findings
- **Effective Range:** AO improves SNR significantly within a "moderate turbulence" range defined by heat transfer ($Q$) between **136 ± 10 J** and **450 ± 70 J**.
- **Failing Point:** Beyond **450 ± 70 J**, the AO system fails to improve SNR, and may degrade it, due to hardware limitations (DM actuator stroke limit of 13.6 µm) and processing latency.
- **Low Turbulence:** Below **136 J**, AO may introduce minor distortions due to non-flat DM surface.
- **Signal Intensity:** AO correction consistently yields higher total signal intensity within the Region of Interest (RoI) across most turbulence levels.

## Experimental Setup
- **Components:**
    - Laser (600 nm)
    - Heating Wire (Simulated turbulence, $3.1 Ω$, $0.9 A$ DC)
    - Deformable Mirror (DM) (Thorlabs AO Kit)
    - Wavefront Sensor (WFS)
    - CCD Camera (Frame rate: ~34.815 fps)
    - 4f Relay systems for optical alignment
- **Turbulence Proxy:** Heat transferred to air $\Delta Q = P \Delta t = I^2 R \Delta t$.

## Data Analysis Parameters
The intensity distribution was fitted to a 2D Gaussian function:
$G(x, y) = \text{offset} + A \exp⁰-⁰\left(-\left[a(x-x_0)^2 + 2b(x-x_0)(y-y_0) + c(y-y_0)^2\right]\right)$

### Optimised Parameters (Reference Video)
**Without AO:**
- Amplitude: 7152.0083
- Center $(x_0, y_0)$: (764.776, 614.056)
- Spread $(\sigma_x, \sigma_y)$: (2.6383, 7.1786)

**With AO:**
- Amplitude: 52897.4846 (Significantly higher peak)
- Center $(x_0, y_0)$: (803.520, 718.401)
- Spread $(\sigma_x, \sigma_y)$: (2.2418, 1.9498) (Tighter focus)

